Subject: A unit without young-cell forms: the nursery stays on, young cells never reach compiled code

What this does
  - qjs_aot_set_form(8) (called at load by a unit generated without young-cell forms, i.e. with no fused construct site) sets y_formless.
  - In that mode a counted reference to a young cell (y_dup_young) promotes the cell at once, so no old object, closure cell or container holds a young cell. The interpreter keeps making young cells (nursery on).
  - The places a register-held young cell could reach compiled code promote it first: the entry of a compiled body from the interpreter (arguments and this in the register file), the interpreter's light calls into compiled code (regvm_t_call_aot, regvm_t_call_method_aot), the native-entry calls in JS_CallInternal and JS_Call, and the return value of JS_CallInternal.
  - QJS_FORMLESS=1 turns the mode on without a unit (testing).

Why we need it
  The young-cell tests and noted dups cost richards 5% and crypto 2% in a unit that makes no young cells itself; with the mode the unit carries the ordinary counted forms.

Soundness
  A form-less unit holds counted references only to promoted cells; units with forms are unchanged. Differential corpus identical with QJS_FORMLESS=1 QJS_Y_STRICT=1.

Failure modes
  A unit with forms and a form-less unit linked in one process: the process runs in the promote-on-dup mode and the form unit's young cells are promoted at counted references.

